yes(563,'C','KOH provides 0.025 × 0.02 = 0.00050 mol OH⁻. A monobasic acid reacts with it in a 1:1 ratio, giving molar mass 0.030/0.00050 = 60 g mol⁻¹. CH₃COOH has this molar mass: 2(12) + 4(1) + 2(16) = 60.',[RX],question='25 cm³ of 0.02 mol dm⁻³ KOH neutralises 0.030 g of a monobasic organic acid of general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH. Which formula represents the acid? [C = 12, H = 1, O = 16]',options=['HCOOH','C₂H₅COOH','CH₃COOH','C₃H₇COOH'],audit='Corrected old D to C, removed following Fehling question. General molar mass is14n+46, so n=1, not old14n+45 and n=3.',check='organic-acid-mass')
no(564,'Actual1995Q48 on PDF46. Atom balance gives C₃H₄, not oldC₃H₆. Propyne is consistent, but propadiene and cyclopropene also share that combustion formula. Source asks identity without a triple-bond premise; hold rather than assert propyne uniquely.',year=1995,answer='C consistent listed compound, C₃H₄ established')
yes(565,'B','Dibutylamine has two butyl groups bonded to nitrogen and one N–H bond, so it is a secondary amine. Methylamine is primary, trimethylamine is tertiary and propylene is a hydrocarbon.',[CARB,REB],year=1995,options=['Propylene','Dibutylamine','Methylamine','Trimethylamine'],audit='Actual1995Q49 on PDF46; stable ID retained. Restored C boundary and amine name spelling.')
yes(566,'D','Alcohol molecules form intermolecular hydrogen bonds through their O–H groups. More energy is needed to separate the molecules, giving relatively high boiling points compared with similar-sized molecules without this hydrogen bonding.',[REB],year=1995,question='The relatively high boiling points of alkanols are mainly due to',options=['Ionic bonding','Aromatic character','Covalent bonding','Hydrogen bonding'],audit='Actual1995Q50 on PDF46; stable ID retained.')
yes(567,'B','Calcium oxide reacts with water to form calcium hydroxide: CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂. Formation of a new substance makes it a chemical change. The reaction releases heat.',[RX],check='lime-hydration')
yes(568,'A','Each solution contains 0.200 × 0.100 = 0.0200 mol solute. Pb²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → PbCl₂ requires two chloride ions per lead ion, so chloride limits the precipitate to 0.0100 mol. M(PbCl₂) = 207 + 2(35.5) = 278 g mol⁻¹, giving 2.78 g.',[RX],year=1999,question='200 cm³ each of 0.1 mol dm⁻³ lead(II) nitrate and hydrochloric acid are mixed. Assuming lead(II) chloride is completely insoluble, what mass precipitates? [Pb = 207, Cl = 35.5]',options=['2.78 g','5.56 g','8.34 g','11.12 g'],audit='Actual1999Q1 on PDF53. Preserved explicitly stated complete-insolubility model; corrected old B to A by limiting reagent.',check='lead-precipitate')
yes(569,'B','The amount of gas is 0.05600/22.4 = 0.00250 mol. Its molar mass is 0.11/0.00250 = 44 g mol⁻¹. Vapour density relative to hydrogen is half the relative molecular mass, so it is 22.00.',[GAS],year=1999,question='56.00 cm³ of a gas at STP weighs 0.11 g. What is its vapour density relative to hydrogen? [Molar gas volume at STP = 22.4 dm³ mol⁻¹]',options=['11.00','22.00','33.00','44.00'],audit='Actual1999Q2 on PDF53. Corrected old A to B and false density units; standard hydrogen reference explicit.',check='vapour-density')
