yes(585,'D','M(Na₂CO₃·10H₂O) = 106 + 180 = 286 g mol⁻¹. The sample contains 2.86/286 = 0.0100 mol carbonate. H₂SO₄ reacts with carbonate in a 1:1 ratio, so the required volume is 0.0100/0.100 = 0.100 dm³ = 100 cm³.',[RX],question='What volume of 0.1 mol dm⁻³ sulfuric acid is needed to react completely with 2.86 g of sodium carbonate decahydrate, Na₂CO₃·10H₂O? [Na = 23, C = 12, H = 1, O = 16]',options=['20 cm³','40 cm³','80 cm³','100 cm³'],audit='Corrected old B/nearest40 claim to exact D. Repaired sulfuric acid name and made intended complete acid-carbonate reaction explicit rather than simple physical dissolution.',check='carbonate-titration')
