yes(594,'D','Independence from [Y] means order zero in Y. Doubling [X] multiplies the rate by four, so 2ⁿ = 4 and n = 2. Therefore R = k[X]²[Y]⁰ = k[X]².',[REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13911?exam_type=jamb&page=195'],question='For 2X(g) + Y(g) → Z(g), the rate of formation of Z is independent of [Y] and quadruples when [X] doubles. Which rate law agrees?',options=['R = k[X][Y]','R = k[X]²[Y]','R = k[X]²[Y]²','R = k[X]²[Y]⁰'],audit='Readable matching Myschool13911 restores missing when[X]doubles premise. Distractor exponents taken from visually complete PDF52, not malformed web notation.',check='rate-orders')
